<h2 id="values">Values, lists and maps</h2>
<p>A Bayan program is a set of typed values. Leave the type off a local when the right-hand side shows it (<code>let name = "Aisha"</code>); write it for an empty collection (<code>List&lt;i32&gt;()</code>). Blocks use braces, not indentation; a statement ends with its line.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">scores = [90, 72, 85]
total = 0
for s in scores:
    total += s
print(f"{len(scores)} scores, total {total}, mean {total / len(scores)}")
ages = {"aisha": 36, "maryam": 45}
print("aisha" in ages, "yusuf" in ages)
for name, age in ages.items():
    print(name, "is", age)</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-basics"/>
<p>Integer division rounds toward zero, so the mean is <code>82</code>, not <code>82.33</code>. Python's <code>/</code> gives a float and Bayan has no <code>//</code>; for a fraction, convert first: <code>f64(total) / f64(scores.length)</code>.</p>
<p>A <code>Map</code> keeps insertion order, like a <code>dict</code>; a missing key panics, as <code>d["x"]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>. For a possibly missing key, <code>tryGet(at:)</code> may give <code>null</code>; finish with <code>??</code>.</p>

<h2 id="structs">Classes become structs</h2>
<p>Bayan has no classes or inheritance: data lives in structs, which are values with default field values, properties (a name with <code>=&gt;</code>, no parentheses) and methods.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">@dataclass
class Account:
    owner: str
    balance: int = 0

    @property
    def is_empty(self):
        return self.balance == 0

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.balance += amount

a = Account("aisha")
a.deposit(50)
b = replace(a, owner="maryam")
a.deposit(25)</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-class"/>
<p>A changing method takes <code>ref this</code> first and cannot be called on a <code>let</code>; without it a method can only read. <code>b</code> is separate: <code>a with { owner: "maryam" }</code> copies <code>a</code> with one field replaced, so the later <code>a.deposit(25)</code> leaves <code>b</code> alone. Two Python names can share one object; Bayan names cannot without a <code>ref</code>.</p>

<h2 id="none">None becomes an optional</h2>
<p>An optional value's type ends in <code>?</code>. The compiler will not treat it as present, so there is no <code>AttributeError: 'NoneType'</code>. Test with <code>is not null</code>, which narrows it in the block, or fall back with <code>??</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">def find(names, prefix):
    for n in names:
        if n.startswith(prefix):
            return n
    return None</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-none"/>
<p><code>find</code>'s second parameter is <code>by string prefix</code>. Every required parameter after the first carries a label, a particle, written at the call: <code>find(names, by: "fa")</code>. The <a href="parameters.html">parameters page</a> lists the particles and how to pick one.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>?.</code> (<code>name?.length</code>); the parser rejects it today (BYN2022). Use <code>is not null</code> or <code>??</code>.</note>

<h2 id="errors">Exceptions become fails and catch</h2>
<p>A failing function lists its error type after the parameters with <code>fails</code>, so a failure is visible. The caller passes one up with <code>try</code> or handles it with <code>catch</code>. Python's <code>except</code> clauses map to <code>catch switch</code> arms, which the compiler checks cover every case.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">class ParseError(Exception): pass

def parse(text):
    if not text:
        raise ParseError("empty")
    return int(text)

try:
    n = parse("")
except ParseError:
    n = 0</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-errors"/>
<p>Calls cover three shapes: <code>catch -1</code> falls back on any failure, <code>catch switch</code> picks a value per case, and the last used <code>-2</code> after <code>.notNumber</code>. The <a href="errors.html">Errors guide</a> covers the rest, including data-carrying errors.</p>

<h2 id="files">Files</h2>
<p>No <code>with</code> block. <code>Io.readText</code> reads and closes the whole file before returning, so nothing to release. Every outside call can fail with <code>IoError</code>: declare it or wrap the call in <code>catch</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">with open(path, "w") as f:
    f.write("one\ntwo\nthree\n")
with open(path) as f:
    for n, line in enumerate(f, 1):
        print(f"{n}: {line.rstrip()}")</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-files"/>
<p>Cleanup beyond one call uses <code>defer</code>: a statement runs at scope end, closest to <code>with</code> or <code>finally</code>. See <a href="memory.html#defer">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h2 id="lists">Comprehensions, sorting and lambdas</h2>
<p>No comprehensions yet; accumulate in a loop. <code>sort()</code> sorts in place, so copy first (<code>xs.copy()</code>) to keep the original; lists own their memory, so a copy is written out.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">xs = [3, 1, 2]
ys = sorted(xs)
squares = [x * x for x in xs]</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-sort"/>
<note>Planned: <code>where</code>, <code>select</code> and <code>sumBy</code> on lists, which take an <code>it</code> lambda. Specified, not in the compiler (<code>xs.where(it &gt; 1)</code> gives BYN2003 today).</note>
<p>A lambda needs a function type and appears where one is expected: <code>apply(5, by: it * 3)</code> or <code>apply(5, by: x =&gt; x - 1)</code>. A bare <code>let f = x =&gt; x + 1</code> is rejected.</p>

<h2 id="match">match becomes switch</h2>
<p>A union lists a value's possible shapes; a <code>switch</code> over it must cover every case, and a case can bind its fields. This replaces a class hierarchy and <code>isinstance</code> chains.</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">match shape:
    case Circle(r): return 3.14 * r * r
    case Rect(w, h): return w * h
    case Point(): return 0.0</code></pre>
<example name="guide-py-match"/>
<p>Add a case to <code>Shape</code> and every <code>switch</code> that missed it stops compiling, deliberately. The leading dot in <code>.circle(let r)</code> means "the case of the type the compiler expects here". Whole numbers print as <code>6</code> and <code>0</code>: a whole-valued float prints without a decimal point.</p>

<h2 id="surprises">What will surprise you</h2>

<h3 id="s-types">Everything has a type, and integers have a size</h3>
<p><code>i64</code> is a 64-bit integer, <code>i32</code> a 32-bit one, <code>u8</code> a byte. Python's <code>int</code> never overflows; Bayan's do, and overflow panics instead of wrapping or growing:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">panic:</span> arithmetic overflow
  at overflow.byn:3</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Smaller integers widen on their own, never narrow; you write <code>i32(a)</code> and decide the value fits.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i32`, found `i64`
  --&gt; narrow.byn:3:18

  3 │     let b: i32 = a
    │                  ^
    │
  help: convert it if the value always fits; `i64` does not convert to `i32` without loss
    │ 3 │     let b: i32 = i32(a)
    │   │                  ++++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Why:</b> silent wraparound or lost bits surface far from their cause; Bayan makes narrowing visible and overflow loud.</p>

<h3 id="s-truth">There is no truthiness</h3>
<p>A condition is a <code>bool</code>; empty lists, zero and <code>null</code> are not false. Write the test you mean: <code>xs.isEmpty</code>, <code>n != 0</code>, <code>x is null</code>.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `bool`, found `i32`
  --&gt; truthy.byn:3:8

  3 │     if n {
    │        ^

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Why:</b> <code>if items:</code> cannot tell "no items" from "items is None"; spelling it removes the guess.</p>

<h3 id="s-copy">Assignment does not alias</h3>
<p>In Python <code>b = a</code> makes two names for one list. A Bayan <code>List</code> owns its memory, so <code>var b = a</code> is an error; the compiler suggests <code>move a</code> (<code>a</code> then gone), <code>a.copy()</code> (independent copy), or <code>ref</code> (shared).</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; copy.byn:3:13

  3 │     var b = a
    │             ^
    │
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
    │ 3 │     var b = move a
    │   │             +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2780</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Numbers and <code>bool</code> copy freely; only storage-owning types such as lists and maps are moved or copied by name.</p>
<p><b>Why:</b> copying a big list costs time and memory, and a shared one can change under you. Bayan makes the choice visible in the source. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-let">let is the default</h3>
<p>A <code>let</code> cannot be assigned twice, and a changing method cannot be called on one.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `x` is immutable
  --&gt; immut.byn:3:5

  3 │     x = 2
    │     ^ declared with `let` on line 2
    │
  help: declare it with `var` if it should change
    │ 2 │     var x = 1
    │   │     +++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2104</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Declare <code>var</code> where a value changes; the compiler warns when a <code>var</code> is never reassigned. Every <code>let</code> is a fact you need not track.</p>

<h3 id="s-strings">Strings are bytes you view, not a sequence you rebuild</h3>
<p>A <code>string</code> is immutable UTF-8 text. <code>length</code> counts bytes and <code>runeCount</code> characters, so <code>"héllo"</code> has length 6 and <code>runeCount</code> 5. Slice by byte offsets, <code>s[0..5]</code> (end excluded); a cut splitting a character panics. There is no <code>+</code> on strings:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  --&gt; concat.byn:3:11

  3 │     print(a + "y")
    │           ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write the parts as one interpolation: `$"{a}{b}"`, or `buf.append($"{a}{b}")` in a loop
    │ 3 │     print($"{a}y")
    │   │           +++++

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2209</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Use interpolation, <code>$"{a}y"</code>, or a <code>StrBuf</code> in a loop. Each <code>+</code> copies the text, and the cost hides in a line that looks free.</p>

<h3 id="s-params">Arguments carry labels</h3>
<p>Python passes any argument by keyword; Bayan requires a label, a particle, on every required argument after the first, from a fixed list: <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>. <code>find(names, by: "gr")</code> says what the second argument is for; call sites read as sentences and swapped arguments are a compile error. See <a href="parameters.html">Parameters and arguments</a>.</p>

<h3 id="s-no">What is not there</h3>
<ul>
<li>No classes, inheritance, decorators, generators or <code>yield</code>; no <code>None</code> outside a <code>?</code> type.</li>
<li>No exceptions: a panic (index out of range, overflow, division by zero) is a bug report, not something you catch.</li>
<li>No <code>import</code> for the basics: <code>Io</code>, <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>print</code> are available, and a directory is a module.</li>
<li>No dynamic features: <code>getattr</code>, <code>eval</code>, run-time fields.</li>
</ul>
<p>Next: <a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a> explains the forms in other programs; <a href="effective.html">Effective Bayan</a> says which to choose.</p>
